# cmd.exe seeks by byte offset when it runs `goto` or `call`, and computes that
# offset assuming CRLF. A batch file checked out with LF endings therefore lands
# mid-line and executes garbage — progressively, one byte further off per line,
# so it fails in a way that looks like the file is corrupt rather than misencoded.
# Forced, not merely normalised, because a clone with core.autocrlf=input would
# otherwise produce a script that cannot run.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts break the other way: CRLF leaves \r on the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf
